{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Oregon's pioneering effort to decriminalize drug possession is ending this Sunday, as a new statute reinstates criminal penalties for holding small quantities of hard substances. Under the updated legislation, HB4002, individuals found with illicit items like fentanyl, heroin, or meth face a choice between formal charges or a treatment pathway. This route requires finishing a behavioral health program and joining a deflection initiative to avoid financial penalties, though personal use remains a misdemeanor carrying up to six months behind bars.\n\n***\n\nThe revised law seeks to empower law enforcement to address public drug use more aggressively while imposing stricter punishments for sales occurring near parks. Although it encourages counties to establish diversion programs that steer individuals toward addiction and mental health support, it does not legally require them to do so. Proponents argue this flexibility allows local governments to tailor solutions to their specific budgets, whereas critics fear it may result in an inconsistent and unfair policy landscape across the region.\n\n***\n\nPortland police chief Bob Day noted that the requirements for qualifying for deflection are quite strict, excluding those with active warrants, violent histories, or other pending charges. If an officer determines a person is eligible, they must contact a specialized dispatch line, which then alerts a mobile outreach team. These teams have a thirty-minute window to arrive and engage with the individual; failure to meet this deadline means the person will be taken into custody immediately, as officers cannot wait indefinitely.\n\n***\n\nWhile the entire **country** watches this shift, the specific **age** of a suspect does not alter the core mechanics of the new arrest protocols. O